another really  important piece of what adobe does we  have a platform called adobe education  exchange there's over a million teachers  on that platform they're sharing ideas  with each other there's project plans  there's lesson plans available for them  or how they can bring creativity into a  math class or into a science class or a  geography class  because what that does is  one it makes learning more interesting  it engages students but most importantly  the teachers can assess what the student  has actually comprehended and learned  through what they create  so  education to us is about making both of  those things possible which is  putting the  products in the hands of the students  and preparing the teachers to teach  creativity and bring creativity into the  classroom  and i definitely want to talk a lot more  about this especially what both adobe  and khan academy hope to do together but  before we go there i do want to focus a  little bit on  your story that's one of the interesting  things about about these conversations i  always love to learn how people  got to where they're going we have a lot  of young people who are watching this a  lot of parents were watching this  so so when you were young in elementary  school middle school did you say i mama  sharma i'm going to be a vp and gm of  adobe creative cloud what did you think  you were going to do  maybe it was that oh my gosh well if you  ask my mom she probably thought i was up  to no good  um i was a very very naughty student  and um  was always you know the person was  trying to distract the teacher in the  classroom and distract uh the students  in the classroom  uh but no i certainly had been curious  i'm sorry  you remember what you did  oh my gosh um  i don't know if the parents and teachers  would appreciate this  but i i used to be the student who would  sit at the back of the classroom and  you know um  throw little aeroplanes this is what we  did in india we made aeroplanes with you  know paper and we threw it uh out in  front of the classrooms i would chew  chalk and then you know throw chalk at  different students that was really bad  no chalk  that's a new one i did not know that  that's the thing that  oh my god yes i was a very naughty  but at some point um  sorry  no no go ahead now i'm going to say you  know but it clearly you you evolved uh  you you're going to say it but at some  point  please keep going  yes at some point um  i think i i realized that uh  it was important for me to pay attention  to learning and um and i think my mother  being a schoolteacher was part of that  um  and  really i think the  the way i'd sum it up  sal it's  it's um  a sequence of events that i didn't  expect would happen  but happened but i think the learning  that i had from my teachers from my  parents from the elders around me  was to be ready to embrace them  and be prepared for them  and be able to um uh you know uh not  just embrace them from the standpoint of  accept what happened but embrace them  and take them forward um you know  there there's been lots of ups and downs  in my um journey to get to here but  what's been consistent is being  confident  having a deep sense of self-worth  and a deep sense of confidence that if  somebody  presents something to me i can embrace  it and i think that comes from the  learning the learning and the teachers  around me and um  all the examples that were around me  that gave me that confidence  yeah so so i mean going back to your  original exam i mean your mother was a  teacher and you still it sounds like at  a young age or maybe not the most  obedient obedient child uh at least in  the in the classroom  do you remember when it kind of clicked  in you was there's particular moment  clearly your mother is a teacher who  played a big role in your life if we're  thinking about teacher appreciation week  but were there moments in your education  where you started saying wow this is  really interesting i'm not passionate  about this or this person is really  invested in me  yeah i think where um  what my mom did for us was it was not  just about what we learned in the  classroom  but she also encouraged us to do uh you  know  different kinds of learning so she put  us into theater  uh i was learning i was on stage at an  early stage i was learning  indian classical music  i was learning a rowing  and a variety of different experiences  that taught me consistently that when i  practiced and when i learned from people  who knew more than me  and who were better than me and i  observed what they did  that i was able to incorporate that  learning into whatever that i was  practicing and i think it was the  extracurricular activities  um and the interest that those teachers  and those mentors  took in me that helped me get better and  i was able to apply that into  uh into the classroom as well  and i think from a teacher's standpoint  when it came to you know just regular  studies um  it was a biology teacher who i had and i  think seventh grade she really made  learning incredibly interesting and this  is again you know many many years back  in india in a government school  uh where i was learning and we had the  resources were limited but the teacher  made it interesting by making us you  know take walks helping helping students  get together in groups and have  conversations about the different plants  we saw the different uh insects we saw  uh you know  bringing art by having us draw what we  were seeing and i think all of that and  the interest that she took in making it  fun and  uh making it non-monotonous and  surprising is what engaged me and helped  me  get more um interested in learning and  and then when i started seeing the  results of what i was doing uh i think  you know got me more serious about  continuing to do my studies  and i'm always curious because you know  when i remember when i was in especially  in high school and even in college you  see people whose careers their  mid-career or they've kind of gotten to  impressive places you're like what is  the path how did they figure that out  that they knew what they know what they  were doing i clearly didn't know what i  was doing  but when what did you think you were  going to do when did you kind of find  your direction did you know you're going  to go into industry going to marketing  did you think you were going to do  something else and when did you figure  that out  yeah so uh when i was in school as i  said i was in theater uh my first uh  dream was to um  actually take acting professionally and  i was in uh theater through school  through college  but then my parents didn't think that  was a good choice  my next choice was to join the indian  navy  because my father was um he's he's a war  hero and i really admired him and i was  like i want to go you know  represent my country and be patriotic  but he then discouraged me from doing  that because at that time women didn't  get great opportunities in the indian  navy you were typically stuck behind a  desk  as opposed to what i wanted to do was be  on the front  so that that's what i wanted to do um  and uh when neither of those worked out  at that time at my age or my batch at  the time everybody was studying to go  abroad  and um and i was like no i don't want to  go abroad i want to you know work for my  country  and i decided to do my masters in  business um because i didn't want to do  science because it looked like science  was too hard and people studied too much  so i kind of made the choices out of  convenience really and ended up doing my  mba  and then after that life just happened  you know i was i think  i think ultimately uh sal  if i were to look back on  what's consistent is  what you learn not just in school  but what values get instilled in you  and i think what's helped me through and  sort of guided me through uh  all the  ups and downs and i've had plenty of  downs as well  um is uh  really sort of hanging on to the values  that matter  and uh and for me it's about doing  what's right uh not what's good  it's about being  consistent and thoughtword in action  and it's those kinds of values that see  you through and i think it's so  important again teachers parents  play such an important role in not just  teaching us  the studies that we'd have to do but  also demonstrating and being the  examples for us when it comes to values  you know i'm curious you know i did not  know this background of yours that you  are essentially the theater kid turned  your corporate leader um  you know it's funny a lot of folks  when i was in college i took a theater  class just on a whim  just thinking it would be a nice change  of pace and i i thought it was  transformational and when i really think  about it it affects how i even  interact with people how i communicate  how aware i am of my own being  yeah  what was the effect of theater and  obviously we're here to talk about  creativity there's a lot of creativity  in theater how is that background i  think given you given you some some some  superpowers in it you know most of the  folks who are corporate leaders in  silicon valley  especially if our stereotypically say  you know coming from south asia they  were probably engineering majors you  know come out here start their life as a  software engineer and then move up  through management but you have a very  very different background how does that  either help to hurt you  i think the way it's helped me primarily  this a couple of things that come to  mind  one is the recognition that you are you  know you alone are not what makes that  play happen  there's you there's a person who pulls  the curtain there's the light there's  you know there's the person behind the  camera  there's a team that's involved and  everybody has to work in um in  in sync  uh it's the production is great uh for  the audience only when everybody is  operating together  um on time on cue  and all of that takes an incredible  amount of practice  and incredible amount of collaboration  um and incredible clarity on what the  outcome of the play needs to be  and that's what work is about  the second thing that i learned is  improvisation because oftentimes the  person you are you know working with  they or your excuse me who you might be  interacting with they might forget their  dialogue you might forget your dialogue  and being able to in the moment uh you  know sort of fill the gaps and let the  story continue  and the third area i think is just the  confidence right of um  being in front of  an audience and that's what business is  about  business is about collaboration it's  about  innovating it's about creative ideas  and it's about selling those ideas  confidently  through  storytelling and so i do think that it's  been something that's helped me  including my fighter spirit  right is that's where risk-taking and  planning um i think was an important  thing  that i really admired about the about  what my dad did  and that's something i found i've  incorporated into my work which is  really being thoughtful about  what the strategy for winning needs to  be  what the operational efficiency and  operational cadence needs to be  all of those  skills that you need in business  yeah you know i've never i've never made  the connection before but hearing you  describe it you're so right i never you  know i feel like theater should be part  of any business program now because  you're absolutely right it's a clear  goal a lot of coordination but as soon  as you're out there some new stuff might  happen you're going to have to improvise  uh and you know it reminds me i actually  was for half a year on in in college on  the uh improv comedy troupe and we used  to have to go to college  and you know you just go on stage with  no planning and you know people throw  out words and scenarios and i have never  gone through more stressful thing in my  life than being 